{# render_scene/v1 — narrate one beat of a playthrough from ONE character's point of view.

   Variables (all required; StrictUndefined makes a missing one fail loud):
     protagonist : str            — whose eyes this beat is seen through
     chapter     : int            — position in the telling
     facts       : list[dict]     — the ONLY canon this scene may use. Each: subject, predicate,
                                    object, quote. Already filtered by the spoiler guard.
     choices     : list[str]      — the option labels the player is about to be offered

   THE CONTRACT THIS TEMPLATE RELIES ON: `facts` has already been filtered to what {{ protagonist }}
   is entitled to know. Nothing withheld is in this prompt, so nothing withheld can leak. The
   instructions below are belt-and-braces on top of that guarantee, never a substitute for it —
   see project_context.md 4.4.
#}
You are narrating one scene of a Dexter novel from the point of view of {{ protagonist }}.

WHAT {{ protagonist | upper }} KNOWS AT THIS POINT (chapter {{ chapter }}):
{% for fact in facts %}
- {{ fact.subject }} {{ fact.predicate }} {{ fact.object }}
  (from the source: "{{ fact.quote | trim | truncate(220) }}")
{% else %}
(Nothing yet. {{ protagonist }} is walking into this cold.)
{% endfor %}

RULES:
1. Write ONLY what {{ protagonist }} could perceive, infer, or remember. This list is exhaustive —
   if something is not above, {{ protagonist }} does not know it and must not act as if they do.
2. Do not resolve the scene. It ends on the beat before a decision.
3. Do not mention, hint at, or foreshadow the options listed below. The player chooses; the
   narration must not lean.
4. Second person, present tense, 120-180 words. Match the novels' dry first-person interiority.
5. No headings, no bullet points, no meta-commentary. Prose only.

{% if choices %}
THE PLAYER IS ABOUT TO CHOOSE BETWEEN (context for pacing only — never name these):
{% for choice in choices %}
- {{ choice }}
{% endfor %}
End the scene at the moment this decision becomes unavoidable.
{% else %}
This is the end of the run. Close the beat with a sense of consequence settling, not a cliffhanger.
{% endif %}
